{
    "opponent_reply": null,
    "user_candidates": ["O-O-O", "h4", "d5"],
    "explain": "That’s a classic and important strategic dilemma—attack or consolidate? According to the analysis, castling queenside (O-O-O), playing h3/h4, or striking in the center with d5 are all viable plans (eval +0.35; best moves: d5, O-O-O, h3). Here’s how you might weigh the options:\n\n1. **Kingside Attack (after O-O-O):** If you castle queenside, your rooks will be connected and your king will be safer on the opposite wing. This gives you the green light to push pawns on the kingside (like h4-h5, g4, etc.), opening lines against Black’s king once they castle kingside. This plan is thematic when you have more space and your pieces are ready to join the attack.\n\n2. **Central Control:** Pushing d5 or e5 can open the center or gain space, but only if it’s safe—meaning your pieces are ready, and you’re not leaving weaknesses. The center is the battleground where you can control both flanks, so strengthening your hold there keeps your options flexible.\n\n3. **When to Attack:** Generally, you should attack on the wing when you have more space there, your opponent’s king is (or will be) on that side, and your pieces can join quickly. If the center is closed or locked, a wing attack is more likely to succeed. If the center is open or can be opened, make sure your own king is safe before launching pawns.\n\nSo, your urge to attack is justified if you’ve castled queenside and Black’s king is on the kingside, but keep an eye on the center—if it opens unexpectedly, your own king could be exposed. Sometimes, preparing the attack with moves like h3 or f3, or even strengthening the center with d5, is the way to go.",
    "ask_user": "Which plan feels more appealing to you here, and why? Would you prefer to castle queenside right away or prepare with a central move?",
    "move_evaluation": null,
    "grounding_citations": [
        "analyze_position: eval +0.35, best move d5, candidate moves O-O-O, h3, f3"
    ]
}

